    "updated_at": "2021-10-03T07:33:04Z",
    "body": "> Thanks, I meant to ask whether there are formats that use XMP and don't support binary EXIF - as XMP requires a lot more characters (the JSON version sounds interesting though)\r\n\r\nJPEG XL, or more precisely its file format standardized recently in ISO/IEC 18181-2 (standard will be published this month), supports Brotli-compressed ISOBMFF boxes (box type `brob` (\"brotli-compressed box\"), first 4 payload bytes determine 'real' box type) which can be used to store XMP metadata in a significantly denser way. It can also be used to compress Exif (since while the TIFF tags Exif uses are binary and quite compact, the payload text strings etc are still uncompressed plaintext). So for the purpose of web delivery of XMP, this should be a good thing. The metadata compression mechanism could also be backported to older media formats; in particular in other ISOBMFF based formats (e.g. MP4, JPEG 2000, AVIF) this should be basically automatically work there too once you have implemented `brob` for JPEG XL.\r\n\r\n(Caveat: this `brob` mechanism was only very recently standardized and it is not yet supported in most tools dealing with metadata; it can be quite cheaply implemented in browsers though since they already depend on brotli in order to support `Content-Encoding: br`)",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
